-- Passage rg9-argument-evidence-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('c393b2cc-acdb-5666-b42a-d028bea358de', 'rg9-argument-evidence-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-argument-evidence'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Did the Music Help the Tomatoes?$q$, $q$Last spring, a ninth grader named Tobias Lindqvist ran an experiment for his biology class. He wanted to know whether playing music helps tomato plants grow. His report claimed that it does, and the results were shared in the school newsletter. A closer look shows why his conclusion needs more support.

Tobias grew two tomato plants from the same packet of seeds. He placed one plant on his bedroom windowsill, where he played classical music for three hours every evening. He kept the other plant in the basement laundry room, where it was quiet. After six weeks, the bedroom plant was eleven inches taller than the basement plant.

The difference was large, but music was not the only thing that differed between the two plants. The windowsill received direct sunlight for much of the day, while the laundry room had one small window. The bedroom was also warmer. Any of these differences could explain why one plant grew taller.

The size of the experiment is another problem. With only one plant in each group, a single unhealthy seed could have changed the results. Scientists usually test many plants in each group so that one unusual plant does not decide the outcome.

None of this means Tobias was wrong to be curious. His question is a good one, and he could test it fairly. He might place two groups of ten plants in identical rooms with the same light, water and temperature, and play music in only one room. If the music group still grew taller, his claim would have far stronger support.

Good science is not just about getting an exciting result. It is about making sure the result could not have come from something else.$q$, 'draft')
