-- Passage rg9-argument-evidence-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('b216a66a-35df-58fb-bf9d-6c70662ac876', 'rg9-argument-evidence-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-argument-evidence'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Leave the Stones Where They Lie$q$, $q$Visitors to Pell Creek Gorge have started a new habit. They pull stones from the creek bed and pile them into towers along the trail, then take pictures beside them. The towers may look peaceful, but the practice harms the creek and should stop.

The stones in a creek are not just decoration. Small fish lay their eggs in the spaces between rocks, and young insects cling to their undersides. When a stone is lifted, the eggs and insects beneath it can be washed away or left to dry in the sun. A biologist at Pell Creek State Park counted the creatures living under twenty stones in an undisturbed section of the creek and found more than three hundred insect larvae.

Stacks also confuse hikers. In some parks, rangers build small rock piles to mark trails where the path is hard to see. At Pell Creek, two hikers last summer followed a line of decorative stacks off the trail and had to be guided back by a ranger.

Some people argue that one small tower cannot do any harm. That may be true of a single tower, but the gorge receives about forty thousand visitors each year. If even a small share of them move a few stones, thousands of stones are disturbed every season.

Others say that stacking stones is a form of art that helps people feel connected to nature. Connection to nature is valuable, but there are ways to enjoy the creek that do not change it, such as photographing the water or sketching the cliffs.

Besides, everyone I know thinks rock towers look silly, so clearly most people already agree with me.

The creek was here long before our cameras. We should leave it the way we found it.$q$, 'draft')
