-- Passage rg9-argument-evidence-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('583997bb-2bdb-5dbc-9d26-d262feeeb181', 'rg9-argument-evidence-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g9-argument-evidence'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Do Video Games Make Better Surgeons?$q$, $q$A news story about the Brayford Medical Center has been shared widely online under the headline "Video Games Make Better Surgeons." The story describes a study in which thirty-four surgery trainees completed a practice task on a computer simulator. The task required them to guide tiny instruments through a model of the human body while watching a screen. Trainees who said they played video games at least three hours a week finished the task faster and made fewer errors than those who did not play.

The headline turns this finding into a bold claim, and the claim goes further than the evidence. The study found that gamers did better, but it did not show that gaming caused them to do better. People who choose to play video games may already have quick hands and good hand-eye coordination. Those same skills could explain both their interest in games and their success on the simulator.

The study also measured performance on a simulator, not in a real operating room. A simulator task is controlled with a screen and a hand control, much like a game. That similarity may give gamers an advantage that disappears when they hold real instruments in a real hospital.

Finally, the trainees reported their own gaming hours. Some may have guessed, and others may have rounded up or down.

None of this means the finding is useless. It raises an interesting question that researchers could test properly. They could randomly assign half of a group of trainees to play a certain game for several weeks while the other half did not, then compare how much both groups improve. If the game players improved more, there would be real evidence that gaming builds surgical skill.

Until then, hospitals should not rewrite their training programs because of one headline.$q$, 'draft')
